What is a thread?

Every program you've written so far has run as a single sequence of steps — one line, then the next, top to bottom. A thread is exactly that: one such sequence of execution. But the moment you want two things to happen at once — serve two web requests, download a file while the interface stays responsive, use all eight of your C-P-U cores — you need more than one thread. And you step into concurrency. This module is about doing that correctly, and about the change in Java twenty-one that makes it dramatically easier.

Let's start with the picture: one process, many threads. When the java virtual machine starts your program, it already has one thread — the main thread, running main. A thread is a path of execution within a process, and a process can hold many. And here's the crucial part: all the threads in a process share the same heap — the same objects, the same static fields. Picture one J-V-M process. Inside it, a shared heap of your objects and static fields, which every thread can see. And then several threads — main, worker-one, worker-two — each with its own call stack, and its own program counter, tracking where it is in the code. So each thread has its own stack, but they all reach into one shared heap. That single fact — private stacks, shared heap — is the source of both concurrency's power, and its every hazard.

Now, two terms people blur, worth separating once: concurrency, and parallelism. Concurrency is structure — a program dealing with many tasks in overlapping time periods. A single C-P-U core switches rapidly between threads, making progress on all of them; they're interleaved, not literally simultaneous. Parallelism is execution — tasks literally running at the same instant, which requires multiple cores. So concurrency is about how you organize independent work; parallelism is about physically running it at once. You can have concurrency on one core, by interleaving, and you need multiple cores for true parallelism. Java's threads give you both: the operating system schedules them across whatever cores exist.

So why use threads — and why have they been hard? Two motivations drive almost all concurrency. The first is responsiveness — don't freeze while waiting. A server handling one request shouldn't block the other thousand; an interface shouldn't lock up during a network call. You overlap the waiting. The second is throughput — finish faster by using every core. A task that splits into independent pieces can run them in parallel; module seven's parallel-stream was one taste of this.

But classic threads carry two long-standing problems that this module confronts. First, they are expensive. A traditional Java thread maps one-to-one to an operating-system thread, and costs around a megabyte of stack — so you can afford only thousands, not millions. That's a real ceiling for a server. Second, the shared heap makes them dangerous. Two threads touching the same object without coordination will corrupt it, in ways that are timing-dependent and maddening to reproduce.

So here's the shape of the module, in two movements. First, the tools for running tasks: raw threads, then the executor-service pool that manages them, then Java twenty-one's virtual threads — the headline change that makes threads so cheap you can have millions — and the modern composition tools, completable-future and structured concurrency. Then, the hard part: the shared-mutable-state problem, and the machinery to tame it — synchronized and locks, atomic variables and concurrent collections, and finally volatile and the memory model that explains why all of it is necessary. The throughline: concurrency multiplies what your program can do — but only if every thread agrees on what the shared heap says.
